# jev — environment template. Copy to `.env`, then `chmod 600 .env`.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# THIS FILE CONTAINS NO CREDENTIALS AND MUST NEVER CONTAIN ANY. It is the
# template; `.env` is the secret. `.env` is gitignored, this file is not
# (see the note at the bottom).
#
# `uv run src/doctor.py` reads `.env` and will tell you what is missing.

# The ONE credential jev uses: the AI Gateway key for the `jev` classifier arm.
# Leave it empty and everything still runs except that arm — `doctor.py`
# records a WARN, not a FAIL, and says so.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT AND MUST STAY ABSENT.
#
# Claude Code is invoked here on the subscription via `claude -p`, never on the
# metered API. `doctor.py` treats a MISSING `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` as the expected
# state and complains if one appears. The metered arms (`opus5`, `haiku45`,
# `sonnet5`) are defined in `config/arms.json` and disabled; enabling them turns
# the headline back into a model-vs-model comparison. See `FINDINGS.md`
# Appendix C.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JEV_HOME is NOT set here, on purpose.
#
# `$JEV_HOME` is where jev itself lives, and the `jev` wrapper resolves it from
# its own location. It is a shell/environment variable, not a project secret,
# and putting a stale absolute path in a copied `.env` is how the global kill
# switch (`$JEV_HOME/.jev-disabled`) ends up naming a file that cannot exist.
# If you need it on `$PATH`, export it from your shell rc instead:
#
#   export JEV_HOME=/path/to/jev
#
# See `docs/REVERSIBILITY.md` § "`JEV_HOME`, and the install shape that killed
# the switch".
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE FOR WHOEVER COMMITS THIS FILE, 2026-09-21:
# `.gitignore` line 3 is `.env.*`, which matches `.env.example` — so this file
# is ignored and `git add` will skip it in silence. It needs a negation:
#
#   !.env.example
#
# added after the `.env.*` line. Until that lands, the README's
# `cp .env.example .env` is still broken on a fresh clone, which is the exact
# defect this file was written to fix.
